  "id": "split-stance-squat",
  "name": "Stationary Split Squat",
  "definition": "A stationary unilateral-emphasis compound exercise performed from a fixed sagittal split stance with a matched pair of side-held dumbbells. Split Squat keeps the rear forefoot on the floor with an approximately leg-length stance, erect torso, and lead-thigh-parallel endpoint. Bulgarian Split Squat rests the top of the rear foot on a bench of source-unreported height and lowers to lead-thigh parallel. Both keep their contacts fixed, log one dumbbell mass, and return through lead-side hip extension, knee extension, and ankle plantarflexion. Dynamic lunges, barbell, bodyweight, single-goblet, Smith, kettlebell, other rear supports, and deficit branches remain outside this active roster. Tibialis anterior is authored as an ankle/foot stabilizer and TFL as a hip/pelvic-control stabilizer. These are categorical anatomy-and-mechanics inferences rather than exact-fixture force or hypertrophy measurements.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "arnold-2010-lower-limb",
    "song-2023-split-squat-step-length",
    "stastny-2015-split-squat-dumbbell-position",
    "usmc-2017-dumbbell-split-squat",
    "nsca-2024-tsac-report-74-dumbbell-split-squat",
    "mackey-2021-bulgarian-split-squat",
    "nsca-2016-division-i-basketball-injury-screening",
    "deforest-2014-single-double-leg-squat",
    "collings-2023-gluteal-muscle-forces"
  ],
